# XBD 11.1 Interface Characteristics
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Every test uses a private pseudo-terminal (XSI): the test process holds the manager side and
# a subsidiary side is read and written, or a child session leader owns it as controlling
# terminal. Tests that need job control run a session leader S as a child with the
# subsidiary side as controlling terminal; S forks the foreground, background and orphaned
# callers. Time bounds are generous and every blocking call is guarded by an alarm, a poll or
# the driver's timeout. Not tested here because other modules do: the terminal functions'
# own behaviour (tcgetpgrp, tcsetpgrp, tcflush, tcflow, tcdrain, tcsendbreak, tcsetattr), the
# foreground group of a new controlling terminal (XSH.tcgetpgrp.003) and the group that has no
# process left (XSH.tcgetpgrp.005).

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.001
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.1
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       open() of a pseudo-terminal subsidiary with O_NONBLOCK returns a
           descriptor (the call is bounded by an alarm); with CLOCAL set in
           the terminal an open() without O_NONBLOCK also returns.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. A pseudo-terminal never makes an open wait
           for a modem, so the test shows only that the call returns; the
           CLOCAL half is skipped when the terminal does not keep CLOCAL
           (control modes of a pseudo-terminal are unspecified, XBD 11.2.4).
test       t/001-open-nonblock.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.002
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.3
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       A child of a session leader that has no controlling terminal
           opens the subsidiary side of a pseudo-terminal without O_NOCTTY:
           the terminal does not become its controlling terminal
           (tcgetpgrp() on it returns -1 with ENOTTY).
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The O_NOCTTY case for a session leader is
           covered by XSH.tcgetpgrp.006 and XSH.posix_openpt.005.
test       t/002-noctty-nonleader.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.003
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.3
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       In a session whose leader has a pseudo-terminal as controlling
           terminal, a child of the leader in the leader's process group and
           a child in a group of its own both have that terminal as their
           controlling terminal: on the inherited descriptor tcgetpgrp()
           gives the leader's group and tcgetsid() gives the leader's
           process ID.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The controlling terminal is acquired by
           opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how a terminal
           is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED.
test       t/003-ctty-inherited.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.004
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.3
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       While a session leader holds a pseudo-terminal as controlling
           terminal, a second session leader (a child that called setsid())
           that opens the subsidiary side without O_NOCTTY does not get it
           as controlling terminal (tcgetpgrp() returns -1 with ENOTTY), and
           the first leader keeps it (tcgetpgrp() still returns its group).
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The controlling terminal is acquired by
           opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how a terminal
           is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED.
test       t/004-ctty-one-session.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.005
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.3
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       A child of the session leader that calls setsid() no longer has
           the pseudo-terminal as controlling terminal (tcgetpgrp() on its
           inherited descriptor returns -1 with ENOTTY); the leader, which
           stays in the old session, still has it (tcgetpgrp() returns its
           group).
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The controlling terminal is acquired by
           opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how a terminal
           is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED.
test       t/005-setsid-relinquish.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.006
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.3
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       A session leader that has closed every descriptor it had for its
           controlling terminal, while a child still has one open, can still
           open the ctermid() pathname and the descriptor is its controlling
           terminal (tcgetpgrp() returns its group).
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The controlling terminal is acquired by
           opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how a terminal
           is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED.
test       t/006-close-not-relinquish.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.007
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.2
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       After the session leader acquires a pseudo-terminal, the
           foreground process group stays the leader's group while children
           create a process group (setpgid), another child joins it, a child
           creates a new session and exits, and the new group's processes
           exit.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The controlling terminal is acquired by
           opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how a terminal
           is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED. The group that has no process left is covered by
           XSH.tcgetpgrp.005.
test       t/007-fg-unchanged.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.008
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.4
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       A process in a background process group (not orphaned) that reads
           its controlling terminal, with SIGTTIN neither blocked nor
           ignored, is stopped by SIGTTIN.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The controlling terminal is acquired by
           opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how a terminal
           is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED. The reader is a child of the session leader in a
           group of its own; it is bounded by an alarm so a missing signal
           is a FAIL, not a hang.
test       t/008-bg-read-sigttin.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.009
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.4
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       A process in a background process group that reads its
           controlling terminal while the reading thread blocks SIGTTIN, and
           again while the process ignores SIGTTIN, gets -1 with EIO, is not
           stopped, and (blocked case) has no SIGTTIN pending.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The controlling terminal is acquired by
           opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how a terminal
           is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED.
test       t/009-bg-read-eio.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.010
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.4
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       A process whose process group is orphaned, with SIGTTIN caught
           (neither blocked nor ignored), that reads its controlling
           terminal gets -1 with EIO and its SIGTTIN handler does not run.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The controlling terminal is acquired by
           opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how a terminal
           is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED. The orphaned group is made by a process that forks a
           grandchild into a group of its own and ends.
test       t/010-orphan-read-eio.c
timeout    40

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.011
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.4
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       The session leader, in the foreground group, with TOSTOP set and
           handlers for SIGTTIN and SIGTTOU, reads three bytes sent from the
           manager and writes three bytes that reach the manager; neither
           signal arrives.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The controlling terminal is acquired by
           opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how a terminal
           is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED.
test       t/011-fg-read-write.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.012
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.4
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.5
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       A process in a background process group (not orphaned), SIGTTOU
           neither blocked nor ignored, that writes to its controlling
           terminal while TOSTOP is set is stopped by SIGTTOU.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The controlling terminal is acquired by
           opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how a terminal
           is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED.
test       t/012-bg-write-tostop.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.013
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.4
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       A process in a background process group that writes to its
           controlling terminal while TOSTOP is clear and SIGTTOU is caught
           writes its byte (it reaches the manager) and the handler does not
           run.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The controlling terminal is acquired by
           opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how a terminal
           is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED.
test       t/013-bg-write-no-tostop.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.014
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.4
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       A process in a background process group that writes to its
           controlling terminal while TOSTOP is set, once with SIGTTOU
           blocked and once with it ignored, writes its byte (it reaches the
           manager), is not stopped, and (blocked case) has no SIGTTOU
           pending.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The controlling terminal is acquired by
           opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how a terminal
           is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED.
test       t/014-bg-write-quiet.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.015
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.4
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       A process whose process group is orphaned, with TOSTOP set and
           SIGTTOU caught, that writes to its controlling terminal gets -1
           with EIO and its SIGTTOU handler does not run.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The controlling terminal is acquired by
           opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how a terminal
           is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED.
test       t/015-orphan-write-eio.c
timeout    40

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.016
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.4
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With TOSTOP clear, a process in a background process group (not
           orphaned) that calls tcsetattr(), tcdrain(), tcflow(TCOON),
           tcflush(TCIFLUSH) or tcsendbreak() on its controlling terminal is
           stopped by SIGTTOU.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The controlling terminal is acquired by
           opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how a terminal
           is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED. The functions' own assertions (for example
           XSH.tcsetattr.011) do not clear TOSTOP first, so they cannot show
           that it is ignored.
test       t/016-setparams-ignore-tostop.c
timeout    60

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.017
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.5
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With O_NONBLOCK clear and no input queued, read() on a pseudo-
           terminal subsidiary in canonical mode, and in non-canonical mode
           with MIN 1 and TIME 0, does not return until a caught signal (an
           alarm after one second) interrupts it: -1 with EINTR.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent.
test       t/017-read-blocks.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.018
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.5
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With O_NONBLOCK set and no input queued, read() on a pseudo-
           terminal subsidiary in canonical mode returns -1 with EAGAIN.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The non-canonical case may instead return 0
           (XBD 11.1.7), so only canonical mode is tested.
test       t/018-nonblock-eagain.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.019
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.5
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With O_NONBLOCK set and the line abcdef (and a newline) queued in
           canonical mode, read(3) returns abc and a following read(100)
           returns the 4 bytes that are left.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. Readiness is awaited with poll().
test       t/019-nonblock-partial.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.020
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.6
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       In canonical mode three bytes without a newline queued at the
           manager do not satisfy a blocking read (an alarm interrupts it:
           -1 with EINTR); once the newline arrives the read returns the
           whole line abc and the newline.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent.
test       t/020-canon-line-wait.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.021
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.6
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With two lines queued in canonical mode a read of 100 bytes
           returns only the first; a line xyz read with four reads of one
           byte each gives x, y, z and the newline.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent.
test       t/021-canon-one-line.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.022
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.6
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.9
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       In canonical mode with VEOL set to a chosen byte, the input ab,
           that byte, cd and a newline is read as two lines: the first ends
           at the EOL byte (ab, with or without the EOL byte itself) and the
           second is cd and the newline.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. Whether the EOL byte is returned with its
           line is not stated (EOF is said to be discarded, EOL is described
           as a delimiter like NL), so both forms are accepted.
           See Q-185.
test       t/022-canon-eol.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.023
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.6
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.9
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       In canonical mode input abc, ERASE, d and a newline is read as
           abd and the newline; ERASE on an empty line has no effect and is
           not queued; ERASE after a completed line does not touch that line
           (still unread); several ERASE characters delete no more than the
           line holds.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent.
test       t/023-canon-erase.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.024
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.6
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.9
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       In canonical mode input abc, KILL, def and a newline is read as
           def and the newline; KILL on an empty line has no effect and is
           not queued; KILL after a completed line does not touch that line
           (still unread); two KILL characters in a row do no harm.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent.
test       t/024-canon-kill.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.025
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.9
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       In canonical mode abc followed by EOF is read as abc without a
           newline; EOF alone at the start of a line makes read() return 0;
           ab, EOF, cd and a newline are read as ab and then cd and the
           newline.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent.
test       t/025-canon-eof.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.026
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.7
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With ICANON clear (MIN 7, TIME 0) the seven bytes a, ERASE, b,
           KILL, c, newline, d sent from the manager are all read back
           unchanged in one read.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent.
test       t/026-noncanon-no-edit.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.027
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.6
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       When fpathconf() gives a value n of MAX_CANON for the terminal
           (at most 4096), a canonical line of n-1 bytes including its
           newline is read back whole.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. NOTINUSE when MAX_CANON has no limit for the
           terminal; a value above 4096 is not tested (UNRESOLVED). The line
           is one byte shorter than the limit so that it fits whether or not
           the newline counts.
test       t/027-canon-max-canon.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.028
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.5
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       When fpathconf() gives a value n of MAX_INPUT for the terminal
           (at most 4096), n bytes sent from the manager to a non-canonical
           subsidiary that is not reading can all be read back unchanged.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. NOTINUSE when MAX_INPUT has no limit; a value
           above 4096 is UNRESOLVED.
test       t/028-max-input.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.029
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.7
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With MIN 4 and TIME 5 two bytes already queued are returned by a
           read of 10 after about half a second (not at once, not never);
           with MIN 4 and TIME 20, four queued bytes are returned at once,
           well before the 2 s timer.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. Timing bounds are generous: 0.3 s to 4 s for
           the expiry, under 1.5 s for the immediate case.
test       t/029-case-a-expiry.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.030
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.7
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With MIN 4 and TIME 3 a read with nothing queued is not ended by
           the timer (an alarm after one second interrupts it); with MIN 5
           and TIME 15, four bytes sent 0.6 s apart are all returned by one
           read, which ends about 1.5 s after the last of them.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The delays are generous (a byte is late by up
           to 0.9 s before the result changes). A late scheduler can only
           make this FAIL; rerun once before acting.
test       t/030-case-a-timer.c
timeout    40

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.031
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.7
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With MIN 3 and TIME 0, two queued bytes do not satisfy a read: it
           stays blocked until an alarm after one second interrupts it (-1
           with EINTR, or the two bytes as a read cut short); after a third
           byte (or three fresh bytes) the read returns exactly the queued
           bytes.
note       The read page lets a read that a signal ends after it took some
           data return the count, so both endings of the interrupted read
           are accepted; it may not end before the alarm. XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent.
test       t/031-case-b.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.032
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.7
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With MIN 0 and TIME 5 a read with nothing queued returns 0 after
           about half a second; with MIN 0 and TIME 20 a read returns the
           single byte that arrives after 0.2 s, long before the 2 s timer.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. Bounds: 0.4 s to 3 s for the empty read,
           under 1.5 s for the byte.
test       t/032-case-c.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.033
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.7
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With MIN 0 and TIME 0 a read with nothing queued returns 0 at
           once; with three bytes queued a read of 2 returns 2, a read of 10
           returns the remaining 1 and the next read returns 0.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. Readiness is awaited with poll() before
           reading data.
test       t/033-case-d.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.034
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.7
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With MIN 0 and TIME 20 a read when one byte is already queued
           returns that byte in under one second (not after the 2 s timer).
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent.
test       t/034-case-c-buffered.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.035
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.9
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With ISIG set, the INTR character sent from the manager makes
           SIGINT arrive once at the session leader and once at a child in
           its (foreground) process group, not at a child in another process
           group of the session, and the INTR byte itself is not read (a
           byte sent after it is the only one read).
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The controlling terminal is acquired by
           opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how a terminal
           is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED. The handlers are installed before the children are
           forked, so all of them have it; each handler writes a letter to a
           pipe.
test       t/035-intr.c
timeout    40

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.036
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.9
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With ISIG set, the QUIT character sent from the manager makes
           SIGQUIT arrive once at the session leader and once at a child in
           its (foreground) process group, not at a child in another process
           group of the session, and the QUIT byte itself is not read (a
           byte sent after it is the only one read).
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The controlling terminal is acquired by
           opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how a terminal
           is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED. The handlers are installed before the children are
           forked, so all of them have it; each handler writes a letter to a
           pipe.
test       t/036-quit.c
timeout    40

