assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.038
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.9
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       Two different sets of values for the seven characters INTR, QUIT,
           ERASE, KILL, EOF, EOL and SUSP can be set on a canonical pseudo-
           terminal with tcsetattr() and are read back unchanged by
           tcgetattr().
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The values avoid the NL and CR bytes and the
           usual START and STOP bytes.
test       t/038-chars-changeable.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.039
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.9
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1988
says       XSI: with ICANON and IEXTEN set, the input a, backslash, ERASE, b
           and a newline is read as one line that still contains the ERASE
           byte (and a and b; the backslash may be dropped); the same for
           KILL, and for EOF, which does not end the read early.
note       The escape is an XSI feature (shaded text). The page does not say
           whether the backslash itself stays in the data, so only the facts
           that a, the special byte and b are all there, in one line, are
           required. XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt()
           and its companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A
           set-up that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent.
           See Q-186.
test       t/039-iexten-escape.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.040
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.9
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With ICANON, ISIG, IEXTEN, IXON, IXOFF, ISTRIP, ICRNL, INLCR,
           IGNCR, PARMRK and INPCK clear and CS8 set, all 256 byte values
           sent from the manager are read back unchanged and in order.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. UNRESOLVED when the terminal does not keep
           CS8 (eight bits per byte cannot be assumed).
test       t/040-iexten-clear-raw.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.041
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.10
anchor     functions/close.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       XSI: when the test process closes the last manager descriptor,
           the session leader that has the subsidiary side as controlling
           terminal (CLOCAL clear, SIGHUP caught) receives SIGHUP.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The controlling terminal is acquired by
           opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how a terminal
           is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED. Closing the last manager descriptor is how a pseudo-
           terminal disconnects (XSH close()). The leader is a child of the
           test process, so the test process holds the only manager
           descriptor; CLOCAL is cleared first where the terminal allows it.
test       t/041-hup-sighup.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.042
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.10
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       XSI: when the test process closes the last manager descriptor,
           the session leader, which has not changed the action for SIGHUP,
           is terminated by SIGHUP within 5 s.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The controlling terminal is acquired by
           opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how a terminal
           is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED. Closing the last manager descriptor is how a pseudo-
           terminal disconnects (XSH close()). The leader is a child of the
           test process, so the test process holds the only manager
           descriptor; CLOCAL is cleared first where the terminal allows it.
test       t/042-hup-terminates.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.043
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.10
class      disputed
when       XSI
says       XSI: after the test process closes the last manager descriptor
           and the session leader ignores SIGHUP, a read of the subsidiary
           side by the leader returns 0 (end-of-file), or -1 with EIO when
           the EIO condition of read() also exists; it does not wait and
           returns no data.
note       disputed (new): the text speaks of a modem disconnect detected by
           the terminal interface and does not say that closing the manager
           side of a pseudo-terminal is one, although the page of close()
           makes it send the same SIGHUP and its rationale wants pseudo-
           terminals to behave as like real ones as possible. The leader's
           group is orphaned and, with the hang-up, no group is the
           foreground group, so the EIO condition may exist and either
           result is accepted. XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up
           (posix_openpt() and its companions are XSI); the requirement
           itself is Base. A set-up that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or
           NOTINUSE when the facility is absent. The controlling terminal is
           acquired by opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how
           a terminal is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED. Closing the last manager descriptor is how a pseudo-
           terminal disconnects (XSH close()). The leader is a child of the
           test process, so the test process holds the only manager
           descriptor; CLOCAL is cleared first where the terminal allows it.
           See Q-184.
test       t/043-hup-read-eof.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.044
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.10
class      disputed
when       XSI
says       XSI: after the test process closes the last manager descriptor
           and the session leader ignores SIGHUP, two writes to the
           subsidiary side by the leader both return -1 with EIO.
note       disputed (new): as for the read after a hang-up, the text speaks
           of a modem disconnect and does not say that closing the manager
           side of a pseudo-terminal is one (see the note of the read
           assertion). XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up
           (posix_openpt() and its companions are XSI); the requirement
           itself is Base. A set-up that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or
           NOTINUSE when the facility is absent. The controlling terminal is
           acquired by opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how
           a terminal is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED. Closing the last manager descriptor is how a pseudo-
           terminal disconnects (XSH close()). The leader is a child of the
           test process, so the test process holds the only manager
           descriptor; CLOCAL is cleared first where the terminal allows it.
           See Q-184.
test       t/044-hup-write-eio.c

assertion  XBD.11.1-interface-characteristics.045
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.11
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       After the subsidiary side writes xyz and the manager sends abc
           (unread), closing the only subsidiary descriptor leaves xyz
           readable at the manager, and after the subsidiary side is opened
           again no byte of abc can be read from it.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The terminal's parameters may be reset by the
           last close, so the mode is set again after the reopen.
test       t/045-close-flush.c
